  "text": "I can't verify the count from memory, and none of the three solutions supplies a checkable argument, so I can't confirm any of the answers 8, 12, or 6.\n\n- **Solution 1 (8):** It asserts the count after describing the setup. It names no quasivarieties beyond vague categories and gives no argument for why exactly eight have AP.\n- **Solution 2 (12):** Its framework is shaky. It claims every quasivariety in SA equals the variety with the same subdirectly irreducibles, and that the only relevant data are finite chains. Quasivarieties generated by infinite chains such as Z, or by infinite families, are not considered, so the lattice it counts may be incomplete. Its table also rests on unsupported claims, such as that S_3 has two embeddings into S_5 and that odd chains of depth ω have AP.\n- **Solution 3 (6):** It is the most concrete, with a parametrization and a case list. But its key steps are asserted rather than shown: the \"mixing obstruction,\" the claim that ISP(S_5) fails AP while the full odd family has AP, and the equivalence of the odd Sugihara family with Gödel algebras. It also has the same gap of ignoring quasivarieties generated by infinite chains.\n\nBecause each solution's structural model may omit quasivarieties, and none of its AP verdicts is justified, I don't have grounds to pick one.\n\nCHOICE: NONE",
